**5. Handling Consistency**

```typescript
// frontend/OrderPage.tsx

export function OrderPage({ orderId }: { orderId: string }) {
  const [order, setOrder] = useState<OrderReadModel | null>(null);
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);
  const [error, setError] = useState<string | null>(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    // Initial load
    fetchOrder(orderId)
      .then(setOrder)
      .catch(err => setError(err.message))
      .finally(() => setLoading(false));

    // Subscribe to updates via WebSocket
    const ws = connectToOrderUpdates(orderId);
    
    ws.onmessage = (event) => {
      const update = JSON.parse(event.data);
      setOrder(prev => ({ ...prev, ...update }));
    };

    return () => ws.close();
  }, [orderId]);

  if (loading) return <OrderSkeleton />;
  if (error) return <OrderError message={error} onRetry={() => fetchOrder(orderId)} />;
  if (!order) return <OrderNotFound />;

  return <OrderDetails order={order} />;
}
```

### Anti Patterns

**1. CQRS Everywhere**

Applying CQRS to everything. Unnecessary complexity. Over-engineering. Use when benefits outweigh costs.

**2. Different Teams for Reads and Writes**

Artificial separation. Integration challenges. Coordination overhead. Work together.

**3. Forgetting about Consistency**

Assuming eventual consistency. Not handling it. Race conditions. Design for it.

**4. Complex Synchronization**

Overly complex projections. Difficult to debug. Hard to maintain. Keep simple.

